{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'control_plane.tenants'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	**Every customer on this installation, and nothing from any of their
	databases** (XIV-58).

	No `_topbar.html.twig`, for the reason XIV-57's placeholder gave and this page
	inherits: that bar is the tenant application's navigation — the customer's
	modules, their company name, their account menu — and every one of those needs
	a resolved tenant, which this host deliberately has not got. The header is the
	control plane's own and lives in `_nav.html.twig` since XIV-101 gave this
	surface a second page — which is the moment this comment used to say to wait
	for, and did.

	**Every value here comes off a `TenantSummary`, never off a `Tenant`.** The
	registry row carries `database_dsn` and an encrypted `database_password`, and
	the way those reach a browser is never a decision somebody makes — it is a
	`|json_encode` into a data attribute, a `dump()` left behind, a serializer
	normalising an entity for a fragment. The controller hands this template a list
	of summaries that do not contain either column, so there is nothing here to
	leak. Read `Xivi\ControlPlane\View\TenantSummary` before adding a variable to
	this page, and `TenantListTest` for the assertion that keeps it true.
#}
{#
	One module of one customer, as the modules cell draws it (XIV-95).

	A macro rather than a copied block, because the cell renders the same line in
	two places — the first few, and the tail behind the disclosure — and the whole
	value of the tail is that it is not a different thing from the head.

	**Everything that could be a colour is also a word.** The disagreement is
	spelled out in the same line as the module key, in the same size as the count
	beside it, because roughly one reader in twelve cannot tell two badge shades
	apart and because a screenshot pasted into a chat carries no hover. The count
	is text for the same reason: XIV-59 put it in a `title`, which a touch screen
	and a screen reader both simply do not have.

	No count is drawn for a module the customer has not got installed — there is
	nothing to count, and a zero there would be a finding about a customer rather
	than an absence of one (§8.11).
#}
{% macro line(module) %}
	<li class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-baseline gap-1">
		<span class="badge text-bg-light font-monospace">{{ module.key }}</span>
		{% if module.records is not null %}
			<span>{{ 'control_plane.usage_records'|trans({'%count%': module.records}) }}</span>
		{% endif %}
		{% if module.installedOnly %}
			<span class="text-body-secondary fst-italic">{{ 'control_plane.module_unrecorded'|trans }}</span>
		{% elseif module.enabledOnly %}
			<span class="text-body-secondary fst-italic">{{ 'control_plane.module_not_installed'|trans }}</span>
		{% endif %}
	</li>
{% endmacro %}

{% block body %}
	{#
		Imported inside the block rather than at the top of the file: a top-level
		import in a template that `extends` another is not in scope inside the
		blocks, which is a Twig rule that costs an afternoon the first time.
	#}
	{% import _self as modules %}

	<main class="container page-shell py-4">
		{#
			The header moved into a partial when the module pricing screen arrived
			(XIV-101). The comment at the top of this file said a nav belonged to
			the moment there was a second page rather than to this one, and that is
			the moment; the partial is where the sign-out link now lives, so the two
			pages cannot drift into having different ones.
		#}
		{{ include('@XiviControlPlane/_nav.html.twig', {current: 'control_plane_home'}) }}

		{#
			This page had nothing to flash until it had a button on it (XIV-108).
			The apology posts to `StalledSignupController` and is redirected back
			here, so the answer to "did it go" has to be readable on this page or
			the write is silent. The partial is the application's own, which is
			what the other four control-plane screens include.
		#}
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		{#
			**The line that makes a stuck tenant answerable from the doorway.**

			Drawn only when there is something to say. A banner that is always
			present, reading "0 customers need attention" on all but three days of
			the year, is the same thing as no banner at all — the reader's eye
			learns to skip the box rather than to read the number in it. So when
			every customer is being served there is nothing here, and the absence is
			the good news.

			It names the slugs rather than counting them alone, because the count on
			its own sends somebody scanning the table for the row it is about, and
			the table is right underneath. Two facts and no click.
		#}
		{% if notServing is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-warning d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					<strong>{{ 'control_plane.not_serving'|trans({'%count%': notServing|length}) }}</strong>
					<div class="small font-monospace">
						{{- notServing|map(t => t.slug)|join(', ') -}}
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			**Who confirmed an address and is still waiting** (XIV-108, §8.14).

			A signup that stops at a stage no retry can get past stops there for
			ever: the commonest cause is an operator taking the translated name by
			hand between the confirmation and the next cron run, and no number of
			runs undoes that. Everything that noticed used to be addressed to the
			operator, and the loudest of those signals was a line in cron mail. The
			person waiting heard nothing at all.

			**Above the table, and drawn only when it is not empty.** This is the
			one section on this page carrying an act somebody has to perform, which
			is why it outranks the customer list rather than sitting under it with
			the refusals; and like the banner above, an empty version of it would
			be furniture the eye learns to skip.

			**The message is printed, not described.** `subject` and `body` are the
			mail's own subject and its own plain-text body, rendered by
			`SignupMailer` in the language the signup recorded, which is why the
			text below may not be English. An operator reads the sentences that
			will arrive and then presses the button; there is no second wording
			anywhere for this page to show instead, which is what makes seeing it
			first a property rather than a promise.

			**Nothing on this page sends anything by itself.** No count, no stage
			and no elapsed time triggers a mail, here or anywhere else in the
			feature. See `Xivi\ControlPlane\Signup\StalledSignups` for why the two
			automatic messages that were considered are both wrong somewhere they
			would fire.
		#}
		{% if stalled is not empty %}
			<section class="mb-4">
				<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.stalled'|trans({'%count%': stalled|length}) }}</h2>
				<p class="small text-body-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.stalled_hint'|trans }}</p>

				{% for signup in stalled %}
					<div class="card mb-2">
						<div class="card-body">
							<div class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-baseline gap-2">
								<strong>{{ signup.companyName }}</strong>
								<span class="badge text-bg-light font-monospace">{{ signup.slug }}</span>
								{#
									Colour and the word, never colour alone: the rule this
									page keeps everywhere, because about one reader in twelve
									cannot tell two badge shades apart and a screenshot
									pasted into a chat carries no hover.
								#}
								<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">
									{{ ('control_plane.stage_' ~ signup.stage.value)|trans }}
								</span>
							</div>

							{#
								The address is drawn because the operator is about to write
								to it, and approving a letter with the envelope covered up is
								not approving it. `StalledSignupListing` carries the argument
								for why §8.11's line about a customer's people does not reach
								this person: they are the platform's own correspondent, not
								somebody's user.

								Beside it, the two facts that say how long this has been
								going on, and no judgement drawn from them. §8.10 refused a
								staleness threshold on the table below on the grounds that a
								tenant stuck for 23 hours is exactly as broken as one stuck
								for 25, and the same holds here: the attempt count and the
								moments are stated, and the reader supplies what they mean.
							#}
							<div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
								<span class="font-monospace">{{ signup.email }}</span>
								&middot;
								{{ 'control_plane.stalled_attempts'|trans({'%count%': signup.attempts}) }}
								&middot;
								{{ 'control_plane.stalled_failed'|trans({'%time%': signup.failedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) }}
								{% if signup.confirmedAt %}
									&middot;
									{{ 'control_plane.stalled_waiting'|trans({'%time%': signup.confirmedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) }}
								{% endif %}
							</div>

							{#
								The message, folded into a disclosure rather than laid open.
								A real control, reachable by keyboard and announced by a
								screen reader, which is the shape XIV-95 chose for the module
								tail on the same page. Folded because five of these at full
								height push the customer table off the screen, and open is
								one click away on the row the operator is acting on.

								`|nl2br` because the body is the plain-text part of a mail:
								its paragraph breaks are newlines, and HTML would otherwise
								run five sentences together into one block that reads nothing
								like what arrives.
							#}
							<details class="mt-2">
								<summary class="small">{{ 'control_plane.stalled_preview'|trans }}</summary>
								{#
									The two class names are for the suite as much as for the
									stylesheet, which is what `module-cell` above is for too:
									`StalledSignupTest` reads the previewed text back out and
									compares it to the text of the mail that goes, and that
									comparison is only worth anything if it can address the
									body on its own rather than by position.
								#}
								<div class="border rounded p-2 mt-2 small">
									<div class="signup-message-subject fw-semibold">{{ signup.subject }}</div>
									<div class="signup-message mt-2">{{ signup.body|nl2br }}</div>
								</div>
								<div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
									{{- 'control_plane.stalled_locale'|trans({'%locale%': signup.locale}) -}}
								</div>
							</details>

							{#
								**One message per person, and the record is what enforces
								it.** Once somebody has been written to the button is gone and
								the row says who sent it and when, so a second operator
								opening this page an hour later cannot apologise to the same
								customer twice. The controller refuses it as well, for the two
								operators who load the page in the same minute.

								There is no second button here, and deliberately: nothing on
								this page renames a signup, releases a name or provisions
								anything. §8.10 keeps lifecycle controls off this screen
								because the commands have refusals a button would have to
								reproduce, and that argument did not change for this section.
							#}
							{% if signup.awaitsAMessage %}
								<form method="post" action="{{ path('control_plane_signup_apology', {id: signup.id}) }}" class="mt-2">
									<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('stalled_signup') }}">
									<button class="btn btn-sm btn-primary" type="submit">
										{{ 'control_plane.stalled_send'|trans }}
									</button>
								</form>
							{% else %}
								<div class="small mt-2">
									<i class="bi bi-check2" aria-hidden="true"></i>
									{{ 'control_plane.stalled_sent_by'|trans({
										'%who%': signup.sentBy,
										'%time%': signup.sentAt|date('Y-m-d H:i'),
									}) }}
								</div>
							{% endif %}
						</div>
					</div>
				{% endfor %}
			</section>
		{% endif %}

		{% if tenants is empty %}
			<div class="card">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.no_tenants'|trans }}</h2>
					<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">
						{{ 'control_plane.no_tenants_hint'|trans }}
						<code>bin/console tenant:provision</code>
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% else %}
			<div class="table-responsive">
				<table class="table align-middle">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'common.name'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.status'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.plan'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.domain'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.created'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.provisioned'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.modules'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.usage'|trans }}</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{#
							Ordered by status first and name second, in the controller — see
							`TenantListController` for the argument. What is left to do here
							is make the grouping visible, because a sort nobody can see reads
							as an arbitrary order.
						#}
						{% for tenant in tenants %}
							<tr class="{{ tenant.servesRequests ? '' : 'table-warning' }}">
								<td>
									<div>{{ tenant.name }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">{{ tenant.slug }}</div>
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										Colour and the word, never colour alone: about one reader in
										twelve cannot tell these hues apart, and a badge whose only
										content is a shade is unreadable in the screenshot somebody
										pastes into a chat.
									#}
									<span class="badge text-bg-{{ tenant.statusVariant }}">
										{{ ('control_plane.status_' ~ tenant.status.value)|trans }}
									</span>
								</td>
								<td>{{ tenant.plan }}</td>
								<td>
									{#
										The primary hostname, which is the one a customer's people
										type. The others are shown because a tenant with several is
										usually mid-rename and the old name is the interesting one;
										a tenant with *none* shows an em dash, and that is not a
										rendering accident — provisioning writes the row before it
										routes a hostname, so a blank here is a run that died in
										between.
									#}
									{% if tenant.primaryDomain %}
										<div class="font-monospace">{{ tenant.primaryDomain }}</div>
										{% if tenant.hostnames|length > 1 %}
											<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">
												{{- tenant.hostnames|slice(1)|join(', ') -}}
											</div>
										{% endif %}
									{% else %}
										<span class="text-body-secondary">&mdash;</span>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td class="font-monospace small">{{ tenant.createdAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</td>
								<td class="font-monospace small">
									{#
										Empty for a tenant that never finished provisioning, and the
										pair of columns is the whole story that a "stuck" flag would
										have flattened into a boolean: created three days ago, never
										provisioned. The reader decides what three days means, which
										is the judgement no threshold in this codebase could make
										honestly.
									#}
									{% if tenant.provisionedAt %}
										{{ tenant.provisionedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}
									{% else %}
										<span class="text-body-secondary">&mdash;</span>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td class="module-cell">
									{#
										**What this customer actually has, and where that disagrees
										with the registry** (XIV-95).

										XIV-58 drew `tenant.enabled_modules` alone and said so, because
										the other list — the one in the customer's own database — needs
										a tenant connection this page still does not open. It is read
										by `tenant:usage:collect` instead, in the same switch it
										already makes to count records, and arrives here as a
										control-plane row like everything else on this page.

										So the same three states as the usage cell, for the same
										reason: an installed-module list with no collection time
										beside it would be a claim about a customer's database made
										by a page that never opened one.

										  * *not collected yet* and *could not be read* — the registry
										    list is drawn, labelled as the registry's, and the cell
										    says the other half is not known. Not shown as agreement,
										    which is what drawing it unlabelled would have implied.
										  * *collected* — every module either source knows about,
										    with its record count, and the disagreements named.

										**A disagreement is information, not a fault.** A module
										installed from the console is something an operator did on
										purpose, and §6.1 makes a customer's own definitions the truth
										once installed. So there is no warning colour, no icon that
										means "wrong" and nothing offering to fix it: the cell reports
										two facts and names the difference between them, and the
										reader decides whether it matters.
									#}
									{% if tenant.usage is null or tenant.usage.failed %}
										{#
											Only one of the two lists is known here, so only one is
											drawn — and it is named, because an unlabelled list of
											module keys in this cell now reads as *what the customer
											has*, which is the one thing it is not.
										#}
										{% for module in tenant.modules %}
											<span class="badge text-bg-light font-monospace">{{ module }}</span>
										{% else %}
											<span class="text-body-secondary">&mdash;</span>
										{% endfor %}
										<div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
											{% if tenant.usage is null %}
												{{- 'control_plane.modules_never'|trans -}}
											{% else %}
												{{- 'control_plane.modules_failed'|trans -}}
											{% endif %}
										</div>
									{% else %}
										{% set reconciled = tenant.reconciledModules %}
										{% set differences = tenant.moduleDifferences %}

										{% if differences > 0 %}
											{#
												Said out loud above the list, because the list itself is
												truncated for a customer with many modules and the one
												thing that must not end up behind a disclosure control
												is that there is something to disclose. Plain secondary
												text and an information icon: this is a finding, and
												nothing on this page should read as an accusation.
											#}
											<div class="small text-body-secondary mb-1">
												<i class="bi bi-info-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
												{{ 'control_plane.modules_differ'|trans({'%count%': differences}) }}
											</div>
										{% endif %}

										{#
											A list rather than a row of badges with a `title` on it.
											XIV-59 put the per-module counts in a tooltip, which is
											invisible on a touch screen and to a screen reader — so the
											answer to *of what* was available to a mouse and to nobody
											else. One module per line, its count in words beside it, and
											a disagreement spelled out in the same line.
										#}
										<ul class="list-unstyled mb-0 small">
											{% for module in reconciled|slice(0, 5) %}
												{{ modules.line(module) }}
											{% else %}
												<li><span class="text-body-secondary">&mdash;</span></li>
											{% endfor %}
										</ul>

										{#
											**Six is the most any customer in this repository can have
											today, and a customer could have more.** A cell that grows
											without limit makes the row it is in unreadable and pushes
											every other column off the screen, so the tail folds into a
											disclosure — a real control, reachable by keyboard and
											announced by a screen reader, which is exactly what the
											tooltip this replaces was not.

											Five is the threshold rather than ten because the point is
											a row that stays a row. The ordering above puts every
											disagreement first, so what folds away is only ever modules
											both sources agree about.
										#}
										{% if reconciled|length > 5 %}
											<details class="small">
												<summary class="text-body-secondary">
													{{- 'control_plane.modules_more'|trans({'%count%': reconciled|length - 5}) -}}
												</summary>
												<ul class="list-unstyled mb-0">
													{% for module in reconciled|slice(5) %}
														{{ modules.line(module) }}
													{% endfor %}
												</ul>
											</details>
										{% endif %}

										<div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
											{{- 'control_plane.modules_collected'|trans({'%time%': tenant.usage.collectedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										**What this customer is using, and how old that is** (XIV-59).

										Three states, drawn as three different things on purpose, because
										the failure mode this cell exists to avoid is somebody reading one
										as another:

										  * *no collection yet* — a customer provisioned an hour ago, or
										    an installation where the cron entry has never run. Not zero:
										    nobody has looked.
										  * *the collection failed* — it was tried, at the time shown, and
										    that customer's database did not answer. Not zero either, and
										    the same distinction XIV-39 drew for a mail that was not sent.
										  * *the figures*, with the moment they were taken underneath
										    them. The timestamp is not decoration. These numbers are as
										    old as the last run and a reader who does not know that will
										    act on March's figures in August, which is worse than being
										    shown nothing.

										Counts and never contents. There is no drill-down from here and
										there is not going to be one — §8.11 argues where that line is and
										why an operator page that showed *what* a customer has would have
										made the control plane a way to read somebody's data without their
										knowledge.
									#}
									{% if tenant.usage is null %}
										<span class="text-body-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.usage_never'|trans }}</span>
									{% elseif tenant.usage.failed %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.usage_failed'|trans }}</span>
										<div class="small text-body-secondary">
											{{- 'control_plane.usage_tried'|trans({'%time%': tenant.usage.collectedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% else %}
										{#
											The totals only. **The breakdown by module used to hang off a
											`title` here** and no longer does (XIV-95): a tooltip is
											invisible on a touch screen and to a screen reader, so the
											answer to *of what* was reaching a mouse and nobody else. It
											is drawn as text in the modules cell instead, beside the
											names it is about — which is where somebody looking for it
											would go first, and means the per-module figures and the
											per-module names are decided once rather than twice.
										#}
										<div>
											{{ 'control_plane.usage_users'|trans({'%count%': tenant.usage.userCount}) }}
											&middot;
											{{ 'control_plane.usage_records'|trans({'%count%': tenant.usage.recordCount}) }}
										</div>
										<div class="small">
											{% if tenant.usage.lastLoginAt %}
												{{- 'control_plane.last_sign_in'|trans({'%time%': tenant.usage.lastLoginAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
											{% else %}
												{{- 'control_plane.never_signed_in'|trans -}}
											{% endif %}
										</div>
										<div class="small text-body-secondary">
											{{- 'control_plane.usage_collected'|trans({'%time%': tenant.usage.collectedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
							</tr>
						{% endfor %}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			**Who did not get as far as being a customer** (XIV-125, §8.12).

			The signup form refuses an address whose provider hands out mailboxes
			nobody keeps, because [XIV-98] turns an accepted signup into a real
			database and a real Postgres role. That refusal is a judgement made by
			a list somebody wrote, and the expensive way for it to be wrong is a
			real business being turned away without ever being told why. This
			section is what makes that reviewable: the provider, how often, and
			when it last happened.

			**Under the table rather than above it, and never as a warning.** The
			banner at the top is about customers who are not being served, which
			is somebody's problem right now. This is a tally an operator reads
			when they wonder whether the list is right, and drawing it in yellow
			would put it in competition with the sentence that is not optional.

			Drawn only when there is something in it, like the banner, and for the
			same reason: a card permanently reading "nobody has been turned away"
			is furniture.

			**No addresses**, because none are stored: see
			`Xivi\ControlPlane\Entity\SignupRefusal`. Counts and a domain, which is
			§8.11's line held one degree further back: this person is not a
			customer and was never going to become one.
		#}
		{% if refusals is not empty %}
			<section class="mt-4">
				<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.refusals'|trans }}</h2>
				<p class="small text-body-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.refusals_hint'|trans }}</p>
				<div class="table-responsive">
					<table class="table table-sm align-middle">
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.refusals_domain'|trans }}</th>
								<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.refusals_attempts'|trans }}</th>
								<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.refusals_first'|trans }}</th>
								<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.refusals_last'|trans }}</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							{% for refusal in refusals %}
								<tr>
									<td class="font-monospace">{{ refusal.domain }}</td>
									<td>{{ refusal.attempts }}</td>
									<td class="text-body-secondary">{{ refusal.firstSeenAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</td>
									<td>{{ refusal.lastSeenAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</td>
								</tr>
							{% endfor %}
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</section>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
